WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.760
In late 1991, almost a thousand kilometres of

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.400
the Barwon and Darling rivers turned green with

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:07.760
cyanobacteria.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.960
In the summer of 2018 to 2019, near

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.760
Menindee on the lower Darling, fish deaths were

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.000
estimated at more than a million.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.720
Those deaths were linked to low flow, heat

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:21.120
and algal blooms that left the water short

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:22.120
of oxygen.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:24.840
So why would extra nutrients end in a

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:27.440
fish kill, and what should chemists measure to

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:28.320
see it coming?

00:00:29.534 --> 00:00:32.093
Nitrogen and phosphorus aren't poisons.

00:00:32.494 --> 00:00:34.893
Algae and plants need them to grow.

00:00:35.214 --> 00:00:37.533
In a lake, growth is usually held back

00:00:37.614 --> 00:00:40.093
by whichever nutrient is in shortest supply for

00:00:40.174 --> 00:00:43.693
what the algae need, called the limiting nutrient,

00:00:43.734 --> 00:00:46.093
and in fresh water that's often phosphorus.

00:00:46.654 --> 00:00:48.253
Add more of it, and you've taken the

00:00:48.334 --> 00:00:49.053
brake off.

00:00:50.267 --> 00:00:52.266
So where does the extra come from?

00:00:52.747 --> 00:00:55.946
After heavy rain, fertiliser washes off paddocks into

00:00:56.027 --> 00:00:56.826
creeks.

00:00:57.067 --> 00:00:59.626
That's a diffuse source, spread over a whole

00:00:59.707 --> 00:01:00.346
catchment.

00:01:00.907 --> 00:01:04.026
Sewage effluent and drains are point sources, one

00:01:04.107 --> 00:01:06.186
outlet you can point to, and they bring

00:01:06.307 --> 00:01:08.186
nutrients and organic waste together.

00:01:08.747 --> 00:01:10.786
Urban stormwater adds more.

00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:13.546
The source matters, because it decides where you

00:01:13.627 --> 00:01:15.626
sample and what the fix is.

00:01:16.834 --> 00:01:19.633
With the brake off, algae multiply into a

00:01:19.714 --> 00:01:22.353
bloom, a green scum you can see from

00:01:22.394 --> 00:01:23.193
the bank.

00:01:23.474 --> 00:01:26.113
In daylight, those algae make oxygen as they

00:01:26.274 --> 00:01:27.553
photosynthesise.

00:01:27.794 --> 00:01:30.833
At night they use oxygen as they respire,

00:01:30.954 --> 00:01:32.833
like every living thing in the water.

00:01:33.154 --> 00:01:35.073
So a bloom can give a high oxygen

00:01:35.154 --> 00:01:37.393
reading at noon, and a much lower one

00:01:37.554 --> 00:01:38.353
at dawn.

00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:42.206
Then the bloom runs out of something, light

00:01:42.287 --> 00:01:45.806
or nutrients, and the algae die and sink.

00:01:45.847 --> 00:01:48.846
Bacteria decompose the dead algae, and as they

00:01:49.047 --> 00:01:52.366
respire they use up dissolved oxygen, the oxygen

00:01:52.527 --> 00:01:54.526
gas dissolved in the water.

00:01:54.607 --> 00:01:56.446
They use it faster than it can dissolve

00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:59.166
back in from the air, especially in warm,

00:01:59.327 --> 00:02:00.526
still water.

00:02:00.607 --> 00:02:03.606
Oxygen falls, and fish suffocate.

00:02:03.967 --> 00:02:06.926
In this oxygen crash, bacteria do most of

00:02:07.007 --> 00:02:09.086
the oxygen-consuming work.

00:02:09.167 --> 00:02:11.966
The danger can continue after the bloom dies,

00:02:12.047 --> 00:02:13.646
which is why it's easy to miss.

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:18.626
This chain is called eutrophication: nutrients enrich the

00:02:18.707 --> 00:02:22.306
water, too much grows, and the oxygen crashes.

00:02:22.867 --> 00:02:24.226
Five words carry it.

00:02:24.627 --> 00:02:28.466
Feed, bloom, die, decay, suffocate.

00:02:29.027 --> 00:02:31.106
And remember why the crash is worse in

00:02:31.187 --> 00:02:31.586
summer.

00:02:32.147 --> 00:02:34.786
Warm water holds less oxygen, the way a

00:02:34.907 --> 00:02:36.466
warm soft drink goes flat.

00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:39.586
So how would you see this coming?

00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:41.026
Not by looking.

00:02:41.587 --> 00:02:44.946
Clear water can still carry dissolved nutrients, salts

00:02:45.027 --> 00:02:47.506
or metals, and a pH of seven says

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.106
nothing about oxygen.

00:02:49.427 --> 00:02:52.066
Water quality is a set of measurements, read

00:02:52.147 --> 00:02:52.906
together.

00:02:53.187 --> 00:02:56.786
Chemists group them as physical, chemical and biological.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.280
Physical parameters come first.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.000
Temperature matters because warm water holds less oxygen.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.680
Turbidity is cloudiness from suspended particles.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.040
Cloudy water blocks light and often means soil

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.840
has washed in, but turbidity can't tell you

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.400
about dissolved poisons or germs.

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:22.560
Chemical parameters include pH, dissolved oxygen and conductivity.

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:26.160
Conductivity rises with dissolved ions, so it's a

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:28.560
quick check for salts, but it can't say

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.160
which ions are there.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.440
For nitrate and phosphate, a reagent turns each

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:36.400
one into a coloured compound, read against standards

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:37.840
just like in the last video.

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:41.440
Or ion chromatography separates the ions in a

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.760
column and measures each one as it comes

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:44.320
out.

00:03:45.534 --> 00:03:48.453
Biological parameters look at living things.

00:03:48.734 --> 00:03:48.893
E.

00:03:49.134 --> 00:03:51.693
coli are gut bacteria, so finding them in

00:03:51.774 --> 00:03:55.813
water means faecal contamination, from sewage or animals.

00:03:56.094 --> 00:03:58.813
Counting algal cells under a microscope shows a

00:03:58.854 --> 00:04:01.053
bloom starting before you can see it from

00:04:01.094 --> 00:04:01.693
the bank.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:04.300
Let's read some data.

00:04:04.580 --> 00:04:06.500
Three sites on a lake, measured on the

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:07.500
same day.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:09.460
Read one column at a time.

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:12.980
Nitrate and phosphate rise from A to C,

00:04:13.020 --> 00:04:15.620
and site B already has about nine times

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:16.900
the phosphate of A.

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:19.940
Dissolved oxygen falls from eight point four to

00:04:20.020 --> 00:04:22.100
three point nine milligrams per litre.

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:26.260
And the observations agree: clear water, then scum,

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:27.300
then dead fish.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.940
So C shows the strongest signs of an

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:33.020
oxygen problem, and B also needs follow-up.

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:35.180
What the table can't show is what the

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:36.740
oxygen does at night.

00:04:37.967 --> 00:04:39.406
So why monitor?

00:04:39.887 --> 00:04:42.926
A number only means something against a guideline

00:04:42.967 --> 00:04:44.126
for that water's use.

00:04:44.607 --> 00:04:47.486
For drinking water, the nitrate guideline is fifty

00:04:47.647 --> 00:04:51.006
milligrams per litre, set to protect babies, but

00:04:51.087 --> 00:04:53.486
far lower nutrient levels can feed a bloom

00:04:53.527 --> 00:04:54.406
in a river.

00:04:54.767 --> 00:04:57.326
Sampling above and below a source finds where

00:04:57.367 --> 00:05:00.486
the nutrients enter, and that decides the fix.

00:05:00.767 --> 00:05:03.166
Monitoring also tells a treatment plant what it

00:05:03.247 --> 00:05:05.646
has to remove, like algae at a drinking

00:05:05.727 --> 00:05:08.766
water intake, or nitrogen and phosphorus in sewage

00:05:08.847 --> 00:05:09.886
before it's released.

00:05:10.367 --> 00:05:13.406
And after any fix, only measurement shows whether

00:05:13.447 --> 00:05:14.606
the water recovered.

00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:16.473
Your turn.

00:05:17.034 --> 00:05:19.513
A creek flows past a dairy farm, and

00:05:19.594 --> 00:05:22.233
water is sampled at three sites.

00:05:22.314 --> 00:05:24.793
Use the data to identify the most likely

00:05:24.874 --> 00:05:26.473
source of the nutrients.

00:05:26.554 --> 00:05:29.913
Then explain why dissolved oxygen is lowest at

00:05:29.994 --> 00:05:32.713
site three, not site two.

00:05:32.874 --> 00:05:34.073
Pause here to answer.

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:46.340
Nitrate, phosphate and E.

00:05:46.620 --> 00:05:49.780
coli all jump between sites one and two,

00:05:49.860 --> 00:05:51.860
so the dairy drain is the most likely

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:53.140
source, and the E.

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:55.060
coli points to animal waste.

00:05:55.620 --> 00:05:58.260
Oxygen is lowest at site three because a

00:05:58.340 --> 00:05:59.620
bloom needs time.

00:06:00.100 --> 00:06:03.380
In that slow pool, algae likely grew, died

00:06:03.460 --> 00:06:06.340
and decayed, and bacteria used the oxygen.

00:06:06.900 --> 00:06:09.940
Expecting the lowest oxygen where nutrients are highest

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:10.980
is the trap.

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:12.840
Last one.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:15.880
A water authority wants early warning that a

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:18.440
drinking water dam is heading toward an algal

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:19.280
bloom.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:23.080
Choose one physical, one chemical and one biological

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:26.440
parameter, and explain what each would show.

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:27.800
Pause here to answer.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:42.120
One good set: water temperature, because warm, still

00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:44.080
water favours blooms.

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:48.280
Phosphate, because rising nutrients come before the bloom.

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.680
And algal cell counts, to catch the bloom

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:51.800
starting.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:54.200
Counting dead fish isn't a warning.

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:56.600
It shows the crash has already happened.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:00.840
So, when you're asked why we monitor water,

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:03.320
trace it from the source, through the bloom,

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.120
to the oxygen crash, then choose evidence of

00:07:06.200 --> 00:07:08.280
all three kinds for the job.

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:11.800
Next, how chemists actually measure that oxygen, and

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:14.360
the test that forecasts how fast it'll be

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:15.080
used up.

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:17.740
Here are the key notes.

00:07:17.980 --> 00:07:19.900
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:20.780
to copy them.
